==Luxembourgish==

===Alternative forms===
* {{l|lb|-elchen}} {{i|see usage notes below}}

===Etymology===
From {{der|lb|gem-pro|*-ikīną}}, {{m|gem-pro|*-ukīną}}, a double diminutive, from {{m|gem-pro|*-ikaz}}, {{m|gem-pro|*-ukaz}} + {{m|gem-pro|*-īnaz|*-īną}}.

===Pronunciation===
* {{IPA|lb|/χen/|[ɕən]}}

===Suffix===
{{head|lb|suffix|plural|-ercher|or|-cher|cat2=diminutive suffixes}}

# {{non-gloss definition|creates a [[diminutive]] noun}}
#: {{suffixusex|lb|[[eng]] [[Kaz]]|eng [[Kätzchen]]|t1=a cat|t2=a little cat, kitten}}

====Usage notes====
* Luxembourgish diminutives, unlike German, keep the gender of the basic word. With few exceptions, diminutives exhibit umlaut if possible. Stems ending in ''-k, -g, -ch, -ng'' use the extended suffix {{m|lb|-elchen}}. Some diminutives are irregular: {{m|lb|Wäin}} → {{m|lb|Wéngchen}}.
* When the syllable immediately before ''-chen'' is stressed, the plural is of the form ''-ercher'', otherwise ''-cher'': {{m|lb|Kätzchen}} → {{m|lb|Kätzercher}}, but {{m|lb|Äppelchen}} → {{m|lb|Äppelcher}}. Nouns with an irregular plural often exhibit a similar irregularity in the plural diminutive: {{m|lb|Hand}} → {{m|lb|Hänn}} and analogously {{m|lb|Händchen}} → {{m|lb|Hännercher}}.

====Derived terms====
{{suffixsee|lb}}

